<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<bpmn:definitions xmlns:bpmn="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/MODEL" xmlns:bpmndi="http://www.omg.org/spec/BPMN/20100524/DI" xmlns:dc="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DC" xmlns:camunda="http://camunda.org/schema/1.0/bpmn" xmlns:di="http://www.omg.org/spec/DD/20100524/DI" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:modeler="http://camunda.org/schema/modeler/1.0" id="Definitions_0g2siwq" targetNamespace="http://bpmn.io/schema/bpmn" exporter="Camunda Modeler" exporterVersion="5.48.0" modeler:executionPlatform="Camunda Platform" modeler:executionPlatformVersion="7.15.0">
  <bpmn:process id="save-request-data-process" name="Save Request Data Process" isExecutable="true" camunda:versionTag="0.1">
    <bpmn:documentation>Creates or updates the servedDocuments record for a legal-service application.
Called as a shared sub-process from ServeLegalProcess (initial create / save /
completed) and VerifyServedDateProcess (post-serve save) — the `method`
variable (POST or PUT) selects which behavior this run performs.

Flow: Copy form data (fetches the current Form.io submission, strips
internal-only fields, builds the API payload) → Get OAuth Token (Keycloak
client-credentials) → Invoke api (creates/updates the servedDocuments
record).

Retry-safety design:
- PUT (save/update) always overwrites with whatever Form.io currently holds,
  so it's naturally idempotent — safe to retry any number of times.
- POST (initial create) relies on the backend enforcing uniqueness on
  applicationId: a retried create that gets HTTP 409 means an earlier attempt
  already succeeded server-side (most likely a client-side timeout that hid
  the original success), and is treated as success rather than a failure.
  This is why "Invoke api" checks status codes explicitly instead of just
  catching exceptions — see its own documentation for detail.

Required incoming variables: applicationId, formUrl, method.
Variables set: payload (filtered Form.io submission), apitoken, result (raw
API response body).

Failure handling: genuine failures (as opposed to the 409-is-success case
above) throw plain JS errors, not BpmnError — intentionally, so they can't
be caught/routed by a boundary event. "Copy form data" has
camunda:asyncBefore="true", which creates the one async job boundary in
this process; neither "Get OAuth Token" nor "Invoke api" has an asyncBefore
of its own (Invoke api's asyncAfter only applies once it has already
succeeded), so both run synchronously within that same job. An uncaught
failure anywhere in this chain — the Form.io fetch, the token request, or
the create/update call — therefore fails that one job and raises a single
Camunda incident. The parent process instance PAUSES right there rather
than ending or silently continuing, since by the time this runs the
customer has already been told their submission was accepted. Once the
underlying issue is fixed, retrying the job (Cockpit or automatic retry)
resumes exactly where it left off. Each error message is prefixed with a
code for diagnosability in the incident view:
  Copy form data: [FORMIO_FORMURL_MISSING], [FORMIO_MISSING_PARAMETER],
    [FORMIO_LOGIN_FAILED], [FORMIO_MISSING_JWT_TOKEN], [FORMIO_FETCH_NON_200]
  Invoke api: [PAYLOAD_MISSING], [SERVEDOCUMENT_INVOKE_FAILED]</bpmn:documentation>
    <bpmn:startEvent id="StartSaveStartEvent" name="start save">
      <bpmn:outgoing>flow_start_to_copyformdata</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:startEvent>
    <bpmn:scriptTask id="InvokeApiTask" name="Invoke api" camunda:asyncAfter="true" scriptFormat="javascript">
      <bpmn:documentation>Creates (POST) or updates (PUT) the servedDocuments record.
The backend returns HTTP 409 on POST when a record for this applicationId
already exists — so if an earlier create timed out client-side after the
write actually succeeded, a retried create gets 409 rather than a
duplicate. 409 on POST is treated as an idempotent success, not a
failure — no separate lookup call needed. PUT is idempotent by nature
(always overwrites with the latest form data), so it needs no special
casing at all.</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>flow_getauthtoken_to_invokeapi</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>flow_invokeapi_to_end</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:script>// ============================================================
  // Script Task: Create/update the servedDocuments record.
  // Engine: GraalJS (JDK 21) — avoid bare Java bean-property access
  // (e.g. obj.prop); call explicit getters/Map.get() instead.
  // ============================================================

  var Logger = Java.type("org.slf4j.LoggerFactory");
  var logger = Logger.getLogger("BPMN_SCRIPT");
  var timestamp = new java.util.Date().toString();
  var appId = execution.getVariable('applicationId');
  var apitoken = execution.getVariable('apitoken');
  var method = execution.getVariable('method');
  var payloadStr = execution.getVariable('payload');

  if (!payloadStr) {
      throw new Error("[PAYLOAD_MISSING] Process variable 'payload' is null — fetch script failed upstream.");
  }

  var system = java.lang.System;
  var servedocumentFullUrl = system.getenv("SERVEDOCUMENT_API_BASE_URL").concat("/api/v1/servedDocuments");
  if (method === "PUT") {
      servedocumentFullUrl = servedocumentFullUrl.concat("?applicationId=", appId);
  }

  logger.debug("*********[Save Request Data] {} :: {} :: {} {} - payload {}", timestamp, appId, method, servedocumentFullUrl, payloadStr);

  try {
      var response = org.camunda.connect.Connectors.getConnector("http-connector")
          .createRequest()
          .method(method)
          .url(servedocumentFullUrl)
          .header("Authorization", "Bearer " + apitoken)
          .header("Accept", "application/json")
          .header("Content-Type", "application/json")
          .payload(payloadStr)
          .execute();

      var statusCode = response.getStatusCode();

      if (statusCode &gt;= 200 &amp;&amp; statusCode &lt; 300) {
          execution.setVariable("result", response.getResponse());
          logger.info("*********[Save Request Data] {} :: {} :: {} succeeded - HTTP {}", timestamp, appId, method, statusCode);
      } else if (method === "POST" &amp;&amp; statusCode === 409) {
          // Uniqueness on applicationId means 409 here can only mean an
          // earlier create already landed — most likely a prior attempt
          // whose response we never saw (client timeout). Not a failure.
          execution.setVariable("result", response.getResponse());
          logger.info("*********[Save Request Data] {} :: {} :: create returned 409 — record already exists, treating as success", timestamp, appId);
      } else {
          throw new Error("[SERVEDOCUMENT_INVOKE_FAILED] Unexpected HTTP " + statusCode + " on " + method + " for applicationId=" + appId);
      }

  } catch (e) {
      logger.error("*********[Save Request Data] {} :: {} :: {} failed: {}", timestamp, appId, method, e.message);
      throw e;
  }</bpmn:script>
    </bpmn:scriptTask>
    <bpmn:endEvent id="EndSaveEndEvent" name="end save">
      <bpmn:extensionElements>
        <camunda:executionListener event="start">
          <camunda:script scriptFormat="javascript">var logger = Java.type("org.slf4j.LoggerFactory").getLogger("BPMN_SCRIPT");
var timestamp = new java.util.Date().toString();

logger.debug("*********[Save Request Data] {} :: {} :: Response - ", timestamp, applicationId, result);</camunda:script>
        </camunda:executionListener>
      </bpmn:extensionElements>
      <bpmn:incoming>flow_invokeapi_to_end</bpmn:incoming>
    </bpmn:endEvent>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="flow_invokeapi_to_end" sourceRef="InvokeApiTask" targetRef="EndSaveEndEvent" />
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="flow_start_to_copyformdata" sourceRef="StartSaveStartEvent" targetRef="copyFormData" />
    <bpmn:serviceTask id="GetOAuthTokenTask" name="Get OAuth Token">
      <bpmn:extensionElements>
        <camunda:connector>
          <camunda:inputOutput>
            <camunda:inputParameter name="url">
              <camunda:script scriptFormat="javascript">var system=java.lang.System;
var  urlKCBase = system.getenv('KEYCLOAK_URL');
var keycloakRealm = system.getenv('KEYCLOAK_URL_REALM');

var urlKCFull = `${urlKCBase}/auth/realms/${keycloakRealm}/protocol/openid-connect/token`;

urlKCFull;</camunda:script>
            </camunda:inputParameter>
            <camunda:inputParameter name="method">POST</camunda:inputParameter>
            <camunda:inputParameter name="headers">
              <camunda:map>
                <camunda:entry key="Content-Type">application/x-www-form-urlencoded</camunda:entry>
              </camunda:map>
            </camunda:inputParameter>
            <camunda:inputParameter name="payload">
              <camunda:script scriptFormat="javascript">var system = java.lang.System;
var clientSecret = system.getenv("KEYCLOAK_CLIENTSECRET");
var clientId = system.getenv("KEYCLOAK_CLIENTID");

var mypayload = `grant_type=client_credentials&amp;client_id=${clientId}&amp;client_secret=${clientSecret}`;

mypayload;</camunda:script>
            </camunda:inputParameter>
            <camunda:outputParameter name="apitoken">${S(response).prop("access_token").value()}</camunda:outputParameter>
          </camunda:inputOutput>
          <camunda:connectorId>http-connector</camunda:connectorId>
        </camunda:connector>
      </bpmn:extensionElements>
      <bpmn:incoming>flow_copyformdata_to_getauthtoken</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>flow_getauthtoken_to_invokeapi</bpmn:outgoing>
    </bpmn:serviceTask>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="flow_getauthtoken_to_invokeapi" sourceRef="GetOAuthTokenTask" targetRef="InvokeApiTask" />
    <bpmn:scriptTask id="copyFormData" name="Copy form data" camunda:asyncBefore="true" scriptFormat="javascript">
      <bpmn:documentation>Fetches the current Form.io submission for this application and builds the
'payload' variable that "Invoke api" sends downstream.

Steps: logs in to Form.io as the root user (FORMIO_ROOT_EMAIL/PASSWORD) to
get an x-jwt-token, GETs the submission at 'formUrl', strips internal-only
fields (currentUser, submitterEmail, submitterFirstName, submitterLastName,
currentUserRoles) that shouldn't be sent to the external API, then stores
the rest as a JSON string in 'payload'.

Always re-fetches the latest submission rather than relying on a value
passed in — this is what makes the PUT (save/update) calls downstream safe
to retry: repeating a PUT just re-applies whatever is currently true, never
a stale snapshot.

Throws a plain JS Error on failure, with an error code prefixed in the
message (see the process documentation for the list) — deliberately not a
BpmnError, so it can't be caught and routed by a boundary event. This task
itself is camunda:asyncBefore="true" — it's the async job boundary for the
whole process, so an uncaught failure here (or in Get OAuth Token / Invoke
api, which run synchronously after it within the same job) raises a
Camunda incident and pauses the parent process instance rather than ending
it or letting it continue silently; it resumes once the underlying issue
is fixed and the job is retried.
</bpmn:documentation>
      <bpmn:incoming>flow_start_to_copyformdata</bpmn:incoming>
      <bpmn:outgoing>flow_copyformdata_to_getauthtoken</bpmn:outgoing>
      <bpmn:script>// ============================================================
  // Script Task: Fetch Form.io Submission → Set 'payload' variable
  // Engine: GraalJS (JDK 21) — avoid bare Java bean-property access
  // (e.g. obj.prop); call explicit getters/Map.get() instead.
  // Output: execution.setVariable("payload", &lt;JSON string&gt;)
  // ============================================================

  var Logger = Java.type("org.slf4j.LoggerFactory");
  var logger = Logger.getLogger("BPMN_SCRIPT");
  var timestamp = new java.util.Date().toString();
  var appId = execution.getVariable('applicationId');

  // Guard: formUrl must exist — set by formsflow ApplicationStateListener
  var formUrl = execution.getVariable("formUrl");
  if (!formUrl) {
      throw new Error("[FORMIO_FORMURL_MISSING] Process variable 'formUrl' is null — cannot fetch Form.io submission.");
  }

  // ── Fields to strip before sending to external API ──────────
  var EXCLUSION_LIST = [
      "currentUser",
      "submitterEmail",
      "submitterFirstName",
      "submitterLastName",
      "currentUserRoles"
  ];

  try {
      // ── 1. Auth ──────────────────────────────────────────────
      var formioToken = getFormioToken();

      // ── 2. Fetch submission ──────────────────────────────────
      var response = org.camunda.connect.Connectors.getConnector("http-connector")
          .createRequest()
          .get()
          .url(formUrl)
          .header("x-jwt-token", formioToken)
          .header("Accept", "application/json")
          .execute();

      if (response.getStatusCode() !== 200) {
          throw new Error("[FORMIO_FETCH_NON_200] Form.io fetch failed: HTTP " + response.getStatusCode() + " for " + formUrl);
      }

      var submission = JSON.parse(response.getResponse());
      var formData = submission.data;

      logger.debug("********* {} :: {} :: Form.io submission fetched — building payload", timestamp, appId);

      // ── 3. Build filtered payload object ────────────────────
      var payloadObj = {};

      for (var key in formData) {
          if (!formData.hasOwnProperty(key)) continue;

          if (EXCLUSION_LIST.indexOf(key) !== -1) {
              logger.debug("********* {} :: {} :: EXCLUDED [{}]", timestamp, appId, key);
              continue;
          }

          payloadObj[key] = formData[key];
          logger.debug("********* {} :: {} :: INCLUDED [{}] type={}", timestamp, appId, key, typeof formData[key]);
      }

      // ── 4. Store as JSON string — InvokeApiTask reads this ──
      var payloadStr = JSON.stringify(payloadObj);
      execution.setVariable("payload", payloadStr);

      logger.info("********* {} :: {} :: payload set — keys={} chars={}",
          timestamp, appId,
          Object.keys(payloadObj).length,
          payloadStr.length
      );

  } catch (e) {
      logger.error("********* {} :: {} :: FATAL — Form.io fetch script failed: {}", timestamp, appId, e.message);
      throw e;
  }

  // ── Auth helper ─────────────────────────────────────────────
  function getFormioToken() {
      var formApi  = java.lang.System.getenv("FORMIO_URL");
      var email    = java.lang.System.getenv("FORMIO_ROOT_EMAIL");
      var password = java.lang.System.getenv("FORMIO_ROOT_PASSWORD");

      if (!formApi || !email || !password) {
          throw new Error("[FORMIO_MISSING_PARAMETER] Missing env vars: FORMIO_URL / FORMIO_ROOT_EMAIL / FORMIO_ROOT_PASSWORD");
      }

      var url = new java.net.URL(formApi + "/user/login");
      var conn = url.openConnection();
      conn.setRequestMethod("POST");
      conn.setDoOutput(true);
      conn.setRequestProperty("Content-Type", "application/json");

      var loginPayload = JSON.stringify({ data: { email: email, password: password } });
        var osw = new java.io.OutputStreamWriter(conn.getOutputStream(), "UTF-8");
        osw.write(loginPayload);
        osw.flush();
        osw.close();

      var status = conn.getResponseCode();
      if (status &lt; 200 || status &gt;= 300) {
          throw new Error("[FORMIO_LOGIN_FAILED] Form.io login failed: HTTP " + status);
      }

      var token = conn.getHeaderField("x-jwt-token");
      if (!token) {
          throw new Error("[FORMIO_MISSING_JWT_TOKEN] Form.io login succeeded but x-jwt-token header is absent.");
      }

      logger.debug("********* {} :: {} :: Form.io token acquired", timestamp, appId);
      return token;
  }</bpmn:script>
    </bpmn:scriptTask>
    <bpmn:sequenceFlow id="flow_copyformdata_to_getauthtoken" sourceRef="copyFormData" targetRef="GetOAuthTokenTask" />
  </bpmn:process>
  <bpmndi:BPMNDiagram id="BPMNDiagram_1">
